1	# Qiskit MCP Server
2	
3	[![MCP Registry](https://img.shields.io/badge/dynamic/json?url=https%3A%2F%2Fregistry.modelcontextprotocol.io%2Fv0.1%2Fservers%2Fio.github.Qiskit%252Fqiskit-mcp-server%2Fversions%2Flatest&query=%24.server.version&label=MCP%20Registry&logo=modelcontextprotocol)](https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io/?q=io.github.Qiskit%2Fqiskit-mcp-server)
4	
5	<!-- mcp-name: io.github.Qiskit/qiskit-mcp-server -->
6	
7	A Model Context Protocol (MCP) server that provides quantum circuit transpilation capabilities using Qiskit's pass managers. This server enables AI assistants to optimize quantum circuits for various hardware targets.
8	
9	## Features
10	
11	- **Circuit Transpilation**: Transpile quantum circuits with configurable optimization levels (0-3)
12	- **Preset Basis Gates**: Support for IBM Eagle, IBM Heron, ion trap, and other basis gate sets
13	- **Topology Support**: Built-in support for linear, ring, grid, and custom coupling maps
14	- **Circuit Analysis**: Analyze circuit complexity without transpilation
15	- **Optimization Comparison**: Compare results across all optimization levels
16	- **Dual API**: Supports both async (MCP) and sync (Jupyter, scripts) usage
17	
18	## Prerequisites
19	
20	- Python 3.10 or higher
21	- [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) package manager (recommended) or pip
22	
23	## Installation
24	
25	### From PyPI (when published)
26	
27	```bash
28	pip install qiskit-mcp-server
29	```
30	
31	### From Source
32	
33	```bash
34	# Clone the repository
35	git clone https://github.com/Qiskit/mcp-servers.git
36	cd mcp-servers/qiskit-mcp-server
37	
38	# Install with uv
39	uv sync
40	
41	# Or with pip
42	pip install -e .
43	```
44	
45	## Quick Start
46	
47	### Running the MCP Server
48	
49	```bash
50	# With uv
51	uv run qiskit-mcp-server
52	
53	# Or directly
54	qiskit-mcp-server
55	```
56	
57	### Claude Desktop Configuration
58	
59	Add to your Claude Desktop configuration (`claude_desktop_config.json`):
60	
61	```json
62	{
63	  "mcpServers": {
64	    "qiskit": {
65	      "command": "uv",
66	      "args": [
67	        "--directory",
68	        "/path/to/qiskit-mcp-servers/qiskit-mcp-server",
69	        "run",
70	        "qiskit-mcp-server"
71	      ]
72	    }
73	  }
74	}
75	```
76	
77	## Usage Examples
78	
79	### Async Usage (MCP Server / FastAPI)
80	
81	```python
82	from qiskit_mcp_server.transpiler import transpile_circuit
83	
84	# Simple Bell state circuit (QASM2 - automatically detected)
85	qasm = """OPENQASM 2.0;
86	include "qelib1.inc";
87	qreg q[2];
88	creg c[2];
89	h q[0];
90	cx q[0], q[1];
91	measure q -> c;
92	"""
93	
94	# Transpile with default settings (optimization level 2, QASM3 format)
95	result = await transpile_circuit(qasm)
96	
97	# Transpile for IBM Heron processor
98	result = await transpile_circuit(
99	    qasm,
100	    optimization_level=3,
101	    basis_gates="ibm_heron",
102	    coupling_map="linear"
103	)
104	```
105	
106	### Using QPY Format
107	
108	```python
109	from qiskit import QuantumCircuit
110	from qiskit_mcp_server import dump_qpy_circuit
111	from qiskit_mcp_server.transpiler import transpile_circuit
112	
113	# Create a circuit programmatically
114	qc = QuantumCircuit(2, 2)
115	qc.h(0)
116	qc.cx(0, 1)
117	qc.measure([0, 1], [0, 1])
118	
119	# Convert to QPY (preserves exact parameters and metadata)
120	qpy_circuit = dump_qpy_circuit(qc)
121	
122	# Transpile using QPY format
123	result = await transpile_circuit(qpy_circuit, circuit_format="qpy")
124	# Result includes transpiled circuit in QPY format (for chaining)
125	transpiled_qpy = result["transpiled_circuit"]["circuit_qpy"]
126	
127	# Chain to another operation using QPY
128	result2 = await transpile_circuit(transpiled_qpy, circuit_format="qpy", optimization_level=3)
129	```
130	
131	### Sync Usage (Scripts, Jupyter)
132	
133	```python
134	from qiskit_mcp_server.transpiler import transpile_circuit, analyze_circuit
135	
136	# All async functions have a .sync attribute
137	result = transpile_circuit.sync(qasm, optimization_level=2)
138	
139	# Analyze circuit without transpiling
140	analysis = analyze_circuit.sync(qasm)
141	print(f"Circuit depth: {analysis['circuit_info']['depth']}")
142	print(f"Two-qubit gates: {analysis['gate_categories']['two_qubit_gates']}")
143	```
144	
145	### Compare Optimization Levels
146	
147	```python
148	from qiskit_mcp_server.transpiler import compare_optimization_levels
149	
150	# Compare all optimization levels (0-3) for your circuit
151	comparison = compare_optimization_levels.sync(qasm)
152	
153	for level in range(4):
154	    result = comparison['optimization_results'][f'level_{level}']
155	    print(f"Level {level}: depth={result['depth']}, size={result['size']}")
156	```
157	
158	**LangChain Integration Example:**
159	
160	> **Note:** To run LangChain examples you will need to install the dependencies:
161	> ```bash
162	> pip install langchain langchain-mcp-adapters langchain-openai python-dotenv
163	> ```
164	
165	```python
166	import asyncio
167	import os
168	from langchain.agents import create_agent
169	from langchain_mcp_adapters.client import MultiServerMCPClient
170	from langchain_mcp_adapters.tools import load_mcp_tools
171	from langchain_openai import ChatOpenAI
172	from dotenv import load_dotenv
173	
174	# Load environment variables (OPENAI_API_KEY, etc.)
175	load_dotenv()
176	
177	# Sample Bell state circuit
178	SAMPLE_BELL = """
179	OPENQASM 3.0;
180	include "stdgates.inc";
181	qubit[2] q;
182	h q[0];
183	cx q[0], q[1];
184	"""
185	
186	async def main():
187	    # Configure MCP client
188	    mcp_client = MultiServerMCPClient({
189	        "qiskit": {
190	            "transport": "stdio",
191	            "command": "qiskit-mcp-server",
192	            "args": [],
193	            "env": {},
194	        }
195	    })
196	
197	    # Use persistent session for efficient tool calls
198	    async with mcp_client.session("qiskit") as session:
199	        tools = await load_mcp_tools(session)
200	
201	        # Create agent with LLM
202	        llm = ChatOpenAI(model="gpt-5.2", temperature=0)
203	        agent = create_agent(llm, tools)
204	
205	        # Run a query
206	        response = await agent.ainvoke(f"Transpile this circuit for IBM Heron: {SAMPLE_BELL}")
207	        print(response)
208	
209	asyncio.run(main())
210	```
211	
212	For more LLM providers (Anthropic, Google, Ollama, Watsonx) and detailed examples including Jupyter notebooks, see the [examples/](examples/) directory.
213	
214	## API Reference
215	
216	### Tools
217	
218	| Tool | Description |
219	|------|-------------|
220	| `transpile_circuit_tool` | Transpile a circuit with configurable optimization |
221	| `analyze_circuit_tool` | Analyze circuit structure without transpiling |
222	| `compare_optimization_levels_tool` | Compare all optimization levels (0-3) |
223	| `load_circuit_from_qasm_tool` | Load a circuit from OpenQASM 2.0 or 3.0 string, returning QPY and metadata |
224	| `export_circuit_to_qasm_tool` | Export a QPY circuit to OpenQASM 2.0 or 3.0 format |
225	| `convert_qpy_to_qasm3_tool` | Convert a base64-encoded QPY circuit to QASM3 |
226	| `convert_qasm3_to_qpy_tool` | Convert a QASM3 (or QASM2) circuit to base64-encoded QPY |
227	
228	### Resources
229	
230	| Resource URI | Description |
231	|--------------|-------------|
232	| `qiskit://transpiler/info` | Transpiler capabilities and documentation |
233	| `qiskit://transpiler/basis-gates` | Available basis gate presets |
234	| `qiskit://transpiler/topologies` | Available coupling map topologies |
235	
236	### Core Functions
237	
238	#### `transpile_circuit(circuit, optimization_level=2, basis_gates=None, coupling_map=None, initial_layout=None, seed_transpiler=None, circuit_format="qasm3")`
239	
240	Transpile a quantum circuit using Qiskit's preset pass managers.
241	
242	**Parameters:**
243	- `circuit`: Quantum circuit as QASM3 string, base64-encoded QPY, or QASM2 string (max 100 qubits, 10,000 gates)
244	- `optimization_level`: 0-3 (default: 2)
245	  - 0: No optimization, only basis gate decomposition (fastest)
246	  - 1: Light optimization with default layout
247	  - 2: Medium optimization with noise-aware layout (recommended)
248	  - 3: Heavy optimization for best results (can be slow for large circuits)
249	- `basis_gates`: List of gate names or preset ("ibm_default", "ibm_heron", etc.)
250	- `coupling_map`: List of edges or topology name ("linear", "ring", "grid", "full")
251	- `initial_layout`: List of physical qubit indices (length must match circuit qubits)
252	- `seed_transpiler`: Random seed for reproducibility
253	- `circuit_format`: Format of the input circuit ("qasm3" or "qpy"). Defaults to "qasm3". When "qasm3" is specified, QASM2 is also accepted as a fallback.
254	
255	**Returns:** Dictionary with original/transpiled circuit info and optimization metrics
256	
257	**Note:** Level 3 optimization can be very slow for circuits with >20 qubits or >500 gates. Use level 2 for faster results with good quality.
258	
259	#### `analyze_circuit(circuit, circuit_format="qasm3")`
260	
261	Analyze circuit structure and complexity.
262	
263	**Returns:** Dictionary with gate counts, depth, and categorization (single/two/multi-qubit gates)
264	
265	#### `compare_optimization_levels(circuit, circuit_format="qasm3")`
266	
267	Compare transpilation results across all optimization levels.
268	
269	**Returns:** Dictionary comparing depth, size, and gates for levels 0-3
270	
271	### Circuit Format Support
272	
273	The server supports two circuit formats for **input**:
274	
275	| Format | Description |
276	|--------|-------------|
277	| `qasm3` | OpenQASM 3.0 string (with QASM2 fallback). Human-readable text format. |
278	| `qpy` | Base64-encoded QPY binary format. Preserves exact parameters and metadata. |
279	
280	**QPY output:** All tools return circuits in QPY format (base64-encoded) for precision when chaining tools/servers.
281	
282	**When to use each format:**
283	- **QASM3** (input): Best for human-readable circuits and initial input
284	- **QPY** (input/output): Best for preserving exact numerical parameters when chaining tools/servers
285	
286	### Converting QPY to Human-Readable QASM3
287	
288	To view a QPY circuit output in human-readable format, use the `qpy_to_qasm3` utility:
289	
290	```python
291	from qiskit_mcp_server import qpy_to_qasm3
292	from qiskit_mcp_server.transpiler import transpile_circuit
293	
294	# Transpile a circuit (returns QPY format)
295	result = transpile_circuit.sync(qasm_circuit, optimization_level=2)
296	qpy_output = result["transpiled_circuit"]["circuit_qpy"]
297	
298	# Convert to human-readable QASM3
299	conversion = qpy_to_qasm3(qpy_output)
300	if conversion["status"] == "success":
301	    print(conversion["qasm3"])
302	```
303	
304	### Converting QASM3 to QPY
305	
306	To convert a QASM circuit to QPY format (for full fidelity when chaining tools), use `qasm3_to_qpy`:
307	
308	```python
309	from qiskit_mcp_server import qasm3_to_qpy
310	
311	qasm_circuit = '''
312	OPENQASM 3.0;
313	include "stdgates.inc";
314	qubit[2] q;
315	h q[0];
316	cx q[0], q[1];
317	'''
318	
319	# Convert to QPY format
320	result = qasm3_to_qpy(qasm_circuit)
321	if result["status"] == "success":
322	    qpy_string = result["circuit_qpy"]
323	    # Use qpy_string with tools that accept QPY input
324	```
325	
326	### Available Basis Gate Sets
327	
328	| Preset | Gates | Description |
329	|--------|-------|-------------|
330	| `ibm_eagle` | id, rz, sx, x, ecr, reset | IBM Eagle r3 (127 qubits, uses ECR) |
331	| `ibm_heron` | id, rz, sx, x, cz, reset | IBM Heron (133-156 qubits, uses CZ) |
332	| `ibm_legacy` | id, rz, sx, x, cx, reset | Older IBM systems (uses CX) |
333	
334	You can also provide a custom list of gate names for other hardware targets.
335	
336	### Available Topologies
337	
338	| Topology | Description |
339	|----------|-------------|
340	| `linear` | Chain connectivity (qubit i ↔ i+1) |
341	| `ring` | Linear with wraparound |
342	| `grid` | 2D grid connectivity |
343	| `heavy_hex` | IBM heavy-hex topology (Eagle/Heron architecture) |
344	| `full` | All-to-all connectivity |
345	
346	## Limits and Performance
347	
348	### Circuit Size Limits
349	
350	To ensure reliable performance, the server enforces the following limits:
351	
352	| Limit | Default | Environment Variable |
353	|-------|---------|---------------------|
354	| Maximum qubits | 100 | `QISKIT_MCP_MAX_QUBITS` |
355	| Maximum gates | 10,000 | `QISKIT_MCP_MAX_GATES` |
356	
357	Circuits exceeding these limits will return an error with a descriptive message.
358	
359	You can override these limits via environment variables:
360	
361	```bash
362	# Allow up to 200 qubits and 50,000 gates
363	export QISKIT_MCP_MAX_QUBITS=200
364	export QISKIT_MCP_MAX_GATES=50000
365	```
366	
367	### Performance Recommendations
368	
369	| Optimization Level | Use Case | Performance |
370	|-------------------|----------|-------------|
371	| 0 | Quick iterations, debugging | Fastest |
372	| 1 | Development, prototyping | Fast |
373	| 2 | Production use (recommended) | Balanced |
374	| 3 | Critical applications, small circuits | Slowest |
375	
376	**Tips:**
377	- Use level 2 for most use cases (best balance of quality and speed)
378	- Use level 3 only when circuit quality is critical AND circuit is small (<20 qubits, <500 gates)
379	- Use level 0 or 1 for rapid prototyping and development
380	- The `compare_optimization_levels` tool helps identify the best level for your specific circuit
381	
382	## Transpilation Stages
383	
384	The Qiskit transpiler processes circuits through six stages:
385	
386	1. **init**: Decompose multi-qubit gates to 1 and 2-qubit operations
387	2. **layout**: Map virtual qubits to physical qubits
388	3. **routing**: Insert SWAP gates for hardware connectivity
389	4. **translation**: Convert to target basis gates
390	5. **optimization**: Reduce gate count and circuit depth
391	6. **scheduling**: Add timing and delay instructions
392	
393	## Testing
394	
395	```bash
396	# Run all tests
397	uv run pytest tests/ -v
398	
399	# Run with coverage
400	uv run pytest tests/ --cov=src --cov-report=term-missing
401	
402	# Run specific test class
403	uv run pytest tests/test_transpiler.py::TestTranspileCircuit -v
404	```
405	
406	## Development
407	
408	```bash
409	# Install dev dependencies
410	uv sync --group dev --group test
411	
412	# Run linting
413	uv run ruff check src tests
414	uv run ruff format --check src tests
415	
416	# Run type checking
417	uv run mypy src
418	
419	# Run all checks
420	./run_tests.sh
421	```
422	
423	## Contributing
424	
425	Contributions are welcome! Please see the [CONTRIBUTING.md](../CONTRIBUTING.md) guide in the root of the repository.
426	
427	## License
428	
429	This project is licensed under the Apache License 2.0 - see the [LICENSE](LICENSE) file for details.
430	

